Note = {2nd International Multi-Conference on Society, Cybernetics and
   Informatics, Orlando, FL, JUN 29-JUL 02, 2008},
Organization = {Int Inst Informat \& System},
Abstract = {The simulation of spatial experience in virtual environments allows
   participants to identify known places, orientate themselves and
   appreciate the scale that is involved. The interactive CAVE and Panorama
   environments theoretically offer better media for visual and spatial
   representation of architecture than traditional drawings and scale
   models.
   The article presents a study which investigates this assertion. The
   study compares aspects of spatial perception in physical environments,
   to its representations in CAVE and Panorama. The design of the
   experiment allowed for a comparison between the responses of those who
   encountered a virtual representation of a building prior to experiencing
   its actual physicality, with those who experienced the reverse: the
   physical building first and then the virtual.
   Results of the present Study in terms of architectural education suggest
   an `ideal sequence' for the learning Curriculum and the notion of a
   measurable thickness of representational media.},
