   together Engineering and Economics (ICEE), Guimaraes, PORTUGAL, MAY
   16-17, 2019},
Organization = {Univ Minho, Sch Engn; Univ Porto, Sch Econ \& Management; UP, Ctr Econ
   \& Finance; CentroAlgoritmi},
Abstract = {Transport sector contributes with 46\% of GHG emissions in Brazil and
   30\% of Rio de Janeiro city emissions. The National Climate Change
   Policy mentions the need to mitigate transport's GHG emissions by
   intensifying the use of public transport. Evaluating the transport
   system through its lifecycle and sustainable dimensions before taking a
   decision is necessary. In order to reduce GHG emissions and to host the
   Olympic and Paralympic games 2016, Rio de Janeiro has invested in new
   Light Rail Vehicles (LRV), Bus Rapid Transit (BRT) and metro lines. This
   paper aims to compare metro and BRT systems through a sustainable
   lifecycle analysis, taking Rio de Janeiro city as a case study focusing
   on BRT Transcarioca and the metro extension Line 4. Environmental
   assessment was conducted according to Life Cycle Assessment (LCA)
   methodology, considering as function unit 1 passenger kilometre
   travelled (pkm). Three indicators of each sustainable dimension
   (environmental, social and economic) were analysed supported by
   multicriteria decision analysis (MCDA). The results show BRT more
   sustainable than metro in the case study assessed, and reinforced the
   need of considering the three dimensions of sustainability in the public
   policy planning and decision making.},
