   TAMPA, FL, FEB 28-MAR 02, 1994},
Organization = {Res \& Training Ctr Childrens Mental Hlth; Univ S Florida; Florida Ment
   Inst, Training Res Serv},
Abstract = {This paper presents findings from a project undertaken to explore the
   implementation of the State of Maine's Intensive Child Case Management
   Program for families with children with severe emotional and behavioral
   disabilities. Interviews with 20 child case management staff were
   conducted for the purpose of describing perceived work roles and
   activities in rural communities, and the extent to which case managers'
   responses reflected a family-strengths based perspective. Thematic and
   content analysis of staff interviews revealed several themes regarding
   staff perceptions of the role of the case manager, the ``ideal{''} work
   activities of the case manager, the barriers to achieving the
   ``ideal{''} work activities, and the ways in which responses reflected a
   family-strengths based perspective. The discussion focuses on
   implications for the provision of intensive child case management
   programs in rural areas and provides recommendations for areas of
   further inquiry.},
