   Buenos Aires, ARGENTINA, MAR 25-27, 2011},
Organization = {Univ Hosp Cleveland, Evenor Armington Fund; Fdn FAPS; Fdn Interacoust},
Abstract = {Visual acuity and motion perception are degraded during head movements
   unless the eyes counter-rotate so as to stabilize the line of sight and
   the retinal image. The vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR) is assumed to
   produce this ocular counter-rotation. Consistent with this assumption,
   oscillopsia is a common complaint of patients with bilateral vestibular
   weakness. Shanidze et al. recently described compensatory eye movements
   in normal guinea pigs that appear to anticipate self-generated head
   movements. These responses effectively stabilize gaze and occur
   independently of the vestibular system. These new findings suggest that
   the VOR stabilizes gaze during passive perturbations of the head in
   space, but anticipatory responses may supplement or even supplant the
   VOR during actively generated head movements. This report reviews these
   findings, potential neurophysiological mechanisms, and their potential
   application to human clinical treatment of patients with vestibular
   disease.},
