Note = {2025 World Environmental and Water Resources Congress, Anchorage, AK,
   MAY 18-21, 2025},
Abstract = {Since 1958, when it was first released, Australian Rainfall and Runoff
   (ARR) has remained one of the most influential and widely used
   guidelines for estimation of design flood flows in Australia. In August
   2024 the guidance on the impacts of climate change on design rainfall
   updated. As stated in Chapter 6, ``Climate Change Considerations{''} in
   Book 1, the ARR ``guidance reflects the contemporary science, is
   applicable across the range of design flood approaches, and facilitates
   consistent application of climate change in design flood estimation.{''}
   Key features include the provision of guidance on increases in rainfall
   intensities from 1 year up to and including the probable maximum
   precipitation (PMP) across storm burst durations from 5 min to 7 days
   under four climate scenarios as well as guidance on changes in rainfall
   losses. The Climate Change Calculator is also described. Two case
   studies are overviewed. The first case study is the Warriewood Valley (a
   coastal catchment undergoing urban intensification) located in the
   Northern Beaches of Sydney, Australia. It is concluded that the evolving
   climate change guidance is significantly reducing the timeframe in which
   a significant increase (30\%) in rainfall volume is expected to occur in
   the Warriewood Valley. The second case study is 155 rural catchments
   across New South Wales. The impacts of climate change on 1\% AEP peak
   flows and 10\% AEP and 1\% AEP flood levels are also outlined.},
