   and Time Perception, Athens, GREECE, OCT 07-08, 2010},
Organization = {European Cooperat Sci \& Technol Action TD0904; Natl Techn Univ Athens,
   Sch Architecture; Inst Language \& Speech Proc},
Abstract = {In this essay, we explain time estimation on the basis of principles of
   self-organization. Timing behavior can be seen as an outcome of the
   coupling and coordination across physiological events, overt behavior,
   and task demands. Such coupling reveals itself in scaling relations
   known as fractal patterns. The self-organization hypothesis posits a
   coherent relation between frequency and amplitude of change, as a single
   coordinated unity, that possess fractal features. Empirical data lend
   support of this hypothesis, initiating a discussion on how fractal
   properties of time estimation can be altered by the interplay of
   voluntary and involuntary control of behavior.},
