Note = {14th International Conference on Intellectual Capital, Knowledge
   Management and Organisational Learning (ICICKM), Hong Kong Polytechn
   Univ, Hong Kong, HONG KONG, DEC 07-08, 2017},
Abstract = {Aim: To examine how organizational culture contributes to stores of
   intellectual capital. Using a competing values framework to characterize
   organizational normative workplace culture, four discrete cultural
   archetypes are assessed with respect to their relationship with
   perceptions of intellectual capital of nursing care in Canadian
   long-term care organizations. Background: Intellectual capital
   accumulations may be impacted by a variety of workplace factors,
   including organizational culture. To date, we know little about how
   discrete workplace cultures contribute to the formation of intellectual
   capital. Methods and Analysis: Data for this study was collected in 2016
   by means of a two-stage mail questionnaire sent to the director of
   nursing care in 1579 long-term care establishments operating in all 10
   provinces and three territories in Canada. In total, questionnaires from
   254 establishments were returned, yielding a net response rate of
   16.8\%. A step-wise hierarchical regression was performed to examine the
   independent contribution that four distinct organizational culture
   archetypes make in predicting intellectual capital stores in nursing
   home resident care staff. Results: Intellectual capital is found to be
   positively associated with all four organization culture archetypes.
   After controlling for facility characteristics, nurse intellectual
   capital was found to be strongly associated (p<.001) with nursing units
   characterized as having a clan culture (valuing teamwork and employee
   relations) and an adhocracy culture archetype (valuing problem-solving
   and risk-taking), moderately associated (p<.01) with having a
   hierarchical archetype (values rules, procedure and structure), yet only
   weakly associated (p<.05) with having a market culture archetype
   (valuing goal-setting and outcome attainment). Organizational culture
   archetypes that emphasize `employee commitment' (clan and
   entrepreneurial culture archetypes) rather than `employee control'
   (hierarchical and market culture archetypes) are more strongly and
   positively associated with perceptions of nursing staff intellectual
   capital. Implications: Managers should recognize the importance of
   culture in their workplaces as it relates to the formation of employee
   intellectual capital. Workplaces with `bottom-up' or
   `commitment-oriented' cultures stressing flexibility and adaptability
   were found to be more likely to report having larger employee
   intellectual capital stores than those with strong `top-down' or
   `control-oriented' cultures stressing organizational stability and
   predictability.},
